# The slack of the buffer that actually absorbs the layer. One row per layer whose absorber
# resolves to a buffer, carrying that one buffer's slack and nothing about the other two.
#
# The slack a layer's shares are held to
#   A layer carries three slacks and names one of them as its absorber. Every statement that the
#   shares a layer holds are bounded by its slack means that one. This relation is
#   layers/absorber.sqlc joined to entries/slacks.sqlc on (filing, layer, buffer), and the third
#   key matters: without it, a layer is compared with every buffer it has.
#
# What dropping the buffer key costs
#   Without it, entries/comparable_slack_shares.sqlc and the rule built on it,
#   checks/slack_unit_mismatch.sqlc, which says "a slack is expressed in the unit of the shares it
#   bounds", would examine every slack in the layer. Most of those pairs are a holder against a
#   buffer that absorbs nothing of theirs, where a differing unit is no defect at all.
#   arithmetic/roster.sqlc names that rule the guard for `shares_bounded`, whose site reads only
#   the absorber, so the guard would examine more than its site.
#
# An inner join to the absorber term
#   The join to pm.buffer_term is inside layers/absorber.sqlc, and it is an inner one. A layer
#   whose absorber term no reader's warrant resolves is not here: nothing can be said about the
#   slack of a buffer nobody identified.
-- layers/absorber.sqlc joined to entries/slacks.sqlc on the buffer the layer actually names.
SELECT a.filing, a.layer, a.buffer,
       a.taxonomy, a.term, a.the_readers_warrant,
       s.low, s.mode, s.high, s.unit, s.absent, s.sized
FROM      (
    :compose(layers/absorber.sqlc)
) a
JOIN      (
    :compose(entries/slacks.sqlc)
) s USING (filing, layer, buffer)
